# Generate retro-run's art.
#
# The upstream javascript-racer ships sprites lifted from OutRun and music that
# may not be redistributed, so this draws its own from scratch. What is NOT
# free is the geometry: every sprite lands at the exact atlas rectangle
# common.js names, because SPRITES.w feeds both collision (Util.overlap) and
# scale (SPRITES.SCALE = 0.3 / PLAYER_STRAIGHT.w). Swap the pictures, keep the
# rectangles, and the game plays identically.
#
# The day-cycle backgrounds must be geometry-identical — the cross-fade
# blends them pixel for pixel, so a ridge line one pixel out would ghost. That
# is enforced structurally: the art is drawn once into a buffer of palette
# KEYS, and each scene is that one buffer handed to `Canvas.Sprite` with a
# different palette. There is no way to draw a scene-specific shape, because
# the drawing code never sees a colour.
#
# The game's own road colours (retro-run.cul's SCENE_COLORS) are the values
# printed at the end of a run, so the road meets the horizon in the colour the
# picture ends in.
#
# Run:  culebra --jit examples/games/retro-run/tools/gen_assets.cul [outdir] [what]
#       (default outdir is ../assets; `what` is `backgrounds` or `sprites`
#       to rebuild only one set while iterating on it)

import atlas from './atlas.cul'

# --- palette -----------------------------------------------------------------
